  "full_response": "### **A Brief History of Cloud Computing: From Mainframes to Serverless**\n\n#### **1. The Era of Mainframes (1950s–1970s)**\nCloud computing traces its roots to **mainframe computers**, which were large, centralized systems used by businesses and governments in the 1950s–1970s. These machines were expensive and required specialized environments, but they introduced the concept of **time-sharing**, where multiple users accessed computing resources remotely via terminals. This was an early form of **resource pooling**, a key principle of cloud computing.\n\n#### **2. The Rise of Client-Server Computing (1980s–1990s)**\nThe 1980s saw the shift to **client-server architecture**, where personal computers (clients) connected to centralized servers. This decentralized computing but still relied on on-premises infrastructure. The **internet’s growth** in the 1990s enabled remote access to servers, paving the way for cloud-like services.\n\n#### **3. The Birth of Virtualization (Late 1990s–Early 2000s)**\nA major breakthrough came with **virtualization technology** (e.g., VMware, 1999), which allowed multiple virtual machines (VMs) to run on a single physical server. This improved efficiency and laid the foundation for **Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)**.\n\n#### **4. The Launch of Modern Cloud Computing (2000s)**\n- **2002:** Amazon Web Services (AWS) launched, initially offering storage and computation services.\n- **2006:** AWS introduced **Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2)** and **Simple Storage Service (S3)**, marking the first true **public cloud** services.\n- **2008:** Google launched **Google App Engine**, a **Platform as a Service (PaaS)** offering.\n- **2010:** Microsoft entered the cloud market with **Azure**.\n\nThese services allowed businesses to **rent computing power** instead of owning physical hardware, reducing costs and increasing scalability.\n\n#### **5. The Expansion of Cloud Services (2010s)**\n- **Software as a Service (SaaS)** exploded with services like **Salesforce, Dropbox, and Office 365**.\n- **Hybrid and multi-cloud** models emerged, allowing businesses to mix private and public clouds.\n- **",
